KW  - Artificial intelligence
KW  - Neuroradiology
KW  - Vision-language foundation models
KW  - Large multimodal models
KW  - Automated radiology report generation
KW  - Computed tomography
KW  - Magnetic resonance imaging
KW  - Systematic review
AB  - Purpose
Rapid advances in artificial intelligence, against a backdrop of radiologist shortages, have spurred interest in automated radiological interpretation. This systematic review examines vision-language foundation models (VLFMs) applied to 3D neuroradiological interpretation, across report generation, visual question answering, classification, and spatial grounding. General-purpose large multimodal models (LMMs) are presented as comparator context.
Methods
Following PRISMA 2020 guidelines, we searched PubMed, Scopus, ArXiv, and Google Scholar (January 2023-March 2026) using a concept-based Boolean string. Included studies developed or evaluated a 3D VLFM on CT or MRI with quantitative performance metrics. Screening and data extraction were performed independently by two reviewers. A total of 24 studies were identified for analysis.
Results
General-purpose LMMs showed substantial deficits on 3D brain CT classification tasks, the best achieving an F1 of only 0.31 for haemorrhage subtyping. By contrast, specialised neuro-CT VLFMs trained on paired datasets performed markedly better on the related but distinct task of report generation; BrainGPT reached a FORTE F1 of 0.71 for clinical content fidelity and a 74% pass rate in a blinded Turing-style evaluation. These metrics reflect different tasks and are not directly comparable. Adoption nonetheless faces multiple barriers: high computational demands for volumetric data, metrics favoring linguistic similarity over clinical correctness, hallucination risks, and unresolved medico-legal questions under the EU AI Act.
Conclusion
Vision-language foundation models show considerable promise for neuroradiological report generation but remain most appropriate as supervised decision-support tools. Continued development of domain-specific architectures, clinically grounded evaluation frameworks, and robust regulatory pathways will be essential before routine clinical integration.
