AB  - Multimodal learning uses different types of input data such as text, images, audio and video to improve the ability of conventional machine learning. Compositional Generalization in this context defines the models’ capacity to generalize to compositions of data they have not seen during training. This survey reviews existing research on how multimodal models achieve this compositionality. It goes through some foundational model design logic, supporting infrastructure, and other key trends in this area. The paper reviews common methodological directions taken and the effect of data design and evaluation strategy on generalization. It integrates them in a structured manner to connect methods, representations, and metrics. It also synthesizes insights across studies through a clear hierarchy in order to find shared patterns, commonly used datasets and tasks, along with their strengths and limitations. Lastly, it compares different approaches and outlines open challenges, aiming to point out gaps and future opportunities for building more interpretable and generalizable multimodal models.
