AB  - The growing shortage of skilled labor in industrial environments demands novel approaches for capturing, retaining, and scaling machine-specific expert knowledge. In this paper, we present a modular knowledge management system powered by Large Language Models (LLMs), designed to dynamically acquire, structure and provide technical knowledge tailored to specific machines. The system is built on a modular architecture combining semantic retrieval, prompt engineering and feedback-driven knowledge refinement. At the core of the system lies a Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) architecture to enable access to both documented knowledge and insights based on machine operators’ experiences. The focus of this paper is placed on the design of a modular knowledge base that integrates heterogeneous sources, including technical documentation, protocols and user-generated feedback. A feedback-driven knowledge retention mechanism continuously enriches the knowledge base by leveraging both implicit user interactions and explicit annotations. To demonstrate the system’s applicability, this paper presents a prototypical use case, where the system is integrated into a human-machine interface (HMI) of a CNC machine tool, to support machine operator guidance, to shorten troubleshooting and to ensure machine-specific knowledge continuity. The proposed approach offers an extensible design for LLM-based knowledge systems in production environments with a focus on technical transparency and integration into human-centered workflows.
