AB  - The construction industry continues to face safety challenges stemming from its complex and dynamic environment, which generates large volumes of underutilized heterogeneous data. Although knowledge graphs (KGs) offer a promising approach to structured representation and reasoning in smart construction safety management (SCSM), existing reviews fall short in analyzing KG-specific attributes, methodologies, and future-oriented implementation pathways. To address these gaps, this study systematically reviews 70 articles using a mixed-methods approach that integrates bibliometric and thematic analysis. Key findings include: (1) SCSM-specific KGs remain limited in scale, largely due to domain-specific constraints; (2) they primarily draw on safety regulations, accident reports, project data, and BIM; and (3) KG construction remains semi-automated, with high-level concept extraction still reliant on expert input. KGs are found to support four main applications: accident analysis, hazard identification, decision support, and safety knowledge management. Methodologically, KGs integrate with technologies such as BIM, digital twins, natural language processing, and computer vision to enhance safety performance analysis and decision-making. Nevertheless, challenges in automation, data integration, dynamic risk inference, and generalizability persist. This review contributes a synthesized analysis of KG traits and their alignment with SCSM needs, highlights research gaps in scalability and technology integration, and suggests future pathways combining advanced AI with novel KG paradigms. It aims to offer structured guidance for researchers and practitioners in advancing KG-enabled safety management.
