AB  - In smart aquaculture, combining sensing modalities – vision, acoustics, and environmental probes – helps overcome single-sensor limits in water-quality monitoring, disease detection, and behavior analysis. This review organizes prior work by modality combinations (including a large language model (LLM)–guided net-pen inspection prototype with a remotely operated vehicle (ROV) and an autonomous underwater vehicle (AUV), synchronized camera–sonar trials that quantify salmon responses and enable counting and sizing in industrial sea cages, a buoy-relayed ROV platform for long-running video and water-quality monitoring in Taiwan, fjord-stratification-aware echo-feeding that adapts appetite triggers over seasons, and a trimodal observatory combining cameras, imaging sonar, and environmental probes), deployment patterns, and aquaculture applications. We compare the strengths of each pairing for core tasks – biomass estimation, infrastructure inspection, and health monitoring – and summarize design choices that matter in practice. Evidence from real-world deployments (e.g., vision-language net-pen inspection, synchronized vision-sonar trials in commercial salmon cages, and fjord-stratification-aware echo-feeding) illustrates gains in robustness and accuracy. We also catalog publicly available multimodal datasets and assess suitability by species, environment, duration, and modality coverage to guide benchmarking and data collection. Finally, we highlight trends in lightweight edge computing, large language model interfaces, and digital-twin integration. Framed around sensor pairings rather than tasks, the review provides an implementation-oriented roadmap for intelligent, resilient, and scalable monitoring systems that bridge recent advances with day-to-day farming needs.
