AB  - Modern smart grids increasingly rely on intelligent decision-support systems to coordinate distributed energy resources, renewable generation, and complex cyber–physical infrastructures. Although recent advances in artificial intelligence (AI) have substantially improved forecasting, optimization, and operational analytics, existing solutions remain largely fragmented, with limited system-level integration of perception, reasoning, coordination, and execution. To address this gap, this study conducts a Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews and Meta-Analyses (PRISMA)-guided systematic review of 61 peer-reviewed papers published between 2019 and 2026. Based on the extracted evidence, an evidence-informed six-layer architectural framework is proposed that integrates physical data acquisition, semantic context abstraction, large language model reasoning, multi-agent coordination, tool-augmented execution, digital twin validation, and cross-layer governance into a unified smart grid intelligence architecture. The review shows that recent research has progressively evolved from isolated predictive models toward cognitive and agent-based decision-support systems; however, no existing framework provides an integrated architecture that simultaneously combines semantic reasoning, engineering tool orchestration, digital twin verification, governance mechanisms, and human oversight. To bridge this gap, the proposed framework establishes a perception–reasoning–action workflow and is further illustrated through an operational use-case scenario demonstrating the interaction between heterogeneous data sources, specialized agents, engineering tools, validation mechanisms, and decision support. The analysis indicates that large language models can significantly enhance contextual reasoning, coordination, explainability, and operational adaptability when deployed as cognitive orchestrators rather than autonomous controllers. Nevertheless, practical deployment remains constrained by challenges including numerical reliability, inference latency, cybersecurity, operational safety, and regulatory compliance, requiring bounded autonomy supported by engineering verification, digital twin simulation, governance policies, and human-in-the-loop supervision. This work provides an evidence-informed architectural foundation for trustworthy next-generation smart grid intelligence and offers a structured roadmap for future research on integrating large language models into real-world energy system operation.
