AB  - Multimodal 3D understanding has rapidly emerged as a critical research area, driven by applications in autonomous navigation, robotics, and augmented reality. Following PRISMA guidelines, we systematically searched four databases and identified 53 studies integrating multimodal large language models (MLLMs) with 3D point cloud data, published between 2023 and 2026. We propose a two-level taxonomy distinguishing 3D scene understanding (indoor, multi-object) from 3D object understanding (single-object, fine-grained), since this distinction shapes both training methodology and applicable tasks. For each level, we review task types, benchmarks, training strategies, feature extraction, and alignment mechanisms, cross-comparing reported results within each task family. Our analysis finds that purpose-built spatial and alignment modules, and training data scale and quality, are stronger predictors of performance than model or parameter size. We further identify evaluation reliability as a systemic, largely unresolved bottleneck: automatic metrics are biased toward short or superficially similar outputs, and LLM-based judges introduce instability, cost, and reproducibility concerns of their own. We conclude with a ranked set of open challenges and recommendations for future research, spanning near-term evaluation fixes, medium-term architectural and data priorities, and long-term infrastructure needs. This survey aims to serve as a foundational reference for those exploring the evolving field of multimodal 3D understanding.
